<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mastanabal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mastanabal was a Numidian prince, one of the three legitimate sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> divided the royal functions after Masinissa's death in 148 BCE, Mastanabal received responsibility for justice. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>, by a concubine, and of Gauda, both later kings of Numidia, and he died before his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mastanabal.jpg" alt="Mastanabal" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_head,_north_African,_300_BC,_Cyrene,_BM_Bronze_268,_143107.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
